From a64e48ad2258f0b8fe9c8c674d3b42170476724f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 18:04:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] What the actor speaks is chosen at creation and decides a
 bilingual part
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The career universe's K3 left languages out because nothing would read one: every
market the slate makes work in works in English. The owner ruled that no reader is a
reason to build the reader, so the readers came first. A share of the slate's parts
(about one in eight, the game's ASSUMPTION) are written for a character who speaks a
named language as well as English, Spanish most often by the game's reckoning. Each is
decided on its own stream keyed by the part's id (the pro.language decider), so no draw
of the slate moves, every id and production stays the one it was, and the generator's
version stays: a part a save was posted before today is kept as posted, with no language.

What the actor speaks is asked on Genres & Background: English always, at native or
fluent (the School and every part are in English), up to six languages, one of them
native, each from the real names the map already used. It is an optional field on the
person; an older save has none and reads as English, natively, so it loads and plays on
as it did. The save schema stays 94.

Each step such a part takes reads it, decided rather than displayed:
- a non-speaker is refused at Submit Yourself in words, an agent doesn't put them up
  (slateReach), and a casting office that cast them before doesn't offer it without an
  audition (directOfferFor);
- casting's fit weighs the weaker of the part's language and the actor's English beside
  the techniques, native above fluent above conversational, so the casting chance and an
  agent's reach move with it;
- a conversational speaker comes to a language part of the preparation short of complete,
  read on believability and execution by the tape, the room, the night and the day's take
  (withLanguage in auditionContext, workContext and unitContext). Work On Your Spanish,
  a new step of pro.prepare, brings it on (an hour on the sides, two on the part, faster
  with Accents And Dialects, which it grows a little), and a booking keeps half of what
  was done on the sides, as the other parts do. Native and fluent speakers prepare none.

Measured on a played career (tests/b27-languages.test.ts): the same seed's fluent
speaker was posted a Mandarin short, taped Exceptional at 95.9 and was booked, while the
English-only twin was posted it and refused with the reason; the conversational twin who
didn't work on the language taped 77.2, held back 31 points on believability by the
language, against 95.9 once it was worked on, and casting's chance fell from 0.644 to
0.422. All 31 mutations of the claims were killed.

The résumé lists every language but native English, the Portfolio tab's profile shows
them, the Opportunities tab's card says what the part asks under The Language, and the
guide's new Languages entry says what languages do. The Résumé and Profile entries, and
the docs of fitOf, eligibilityProblems and slateReach, said less than is now true and
were brought up to it.

